#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128839 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129242 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "747f2317-3665-5857-9767-e602f711d6c6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Horizontal Asymptotes of Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "39c2f708-4962-5ed3-9f00-0906f8b34ad2" :question/math-academy-id "e-1127" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the graph shown below?\n\nI. $y=2$ is a horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$\n\nII. $f(x) \\rightarrow -1$ as $x \\rightarrow -\\infty$\n\nIII. $y=0$ is a horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$\n\n![](images/68/68123260b68bd3696d10948e30404534cc6b1b189ce6d8b680a2b616c9712ff3.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement one-by-one.\n\n- On the right side of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets larger and larger, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $2.$ Symbolically, \n\n$$\nf(x) \\to 2 \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\rightarrow \\infty.\n$$\n\n This means that $y=2$ is a horizontal asymptote of $f.$ Therefore, I is true.\n\n- On the left side of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets more and more negative, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $-1.$ Symbolically, \n\n$$\nf(x) \\to -1 \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\to -\\infty.\n$$\n\n Therefore, II is also true.\n\n- The horizontal asymptotes of $f(x)$ are $y=2$ and $y=-1$. The line $y=0$ is not a horizontal asymptote because the function does not level off to $0$ on the right nor on the left. Therefore, III is false.\n\nAs a result, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128840 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129243 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c271d00-a2fd-5665-bed0-f1f298296bd0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Missing Values in End Behavior Statements"}] :question/id #uuid "2d17837e-a77b-5196-8f17-df58075174a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-1129" :question/problem "The graph of the function $y = f(x)$ is given below. Find $a$ and $b$ such that $f(x) \\rightarrow a$ as $x \\rightarrow \\infty$ and $f(x) \\rightarrow b$ as $x  \\rightarrow -\\infty.$\n\n![](images/77/77305c6fdeb2b6f3cc0b427924e418938c07caac79843cc6992da799675aad4f.png)" :question/worked-solution "- On the right of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets larger and larger, the function $f(x)$ grows larger and larger without bound (as indicated by the arrow). We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x) \\rightarrow \\infty \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x\\rightarrow \\infty\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a = \\infty.$\n\n- On the left of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets more and more negative, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $-2.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x) \\rightarrow -2 \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x\\rightarrow -\\infty\n$$\n\nTherefore, $b = -2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507407 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129242 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "747f2317-3665-5857-9767-e602f711d6c6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Horizontal Asymptotes of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e34ee8f-cacd-562e-b3cd-04de565ea24c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506705} {:answer/id #uuid "149a75ef-d1f0-50c8-ad55-673d88e941c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506719} {:answer/id #uuid "33166053-6e7e-5175-8073-b22b63728622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506800} {:answer/id #uuid "a16098d0-0dad-5305-9e69-e9a8a62050cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507025} {:answer/id #uuid "ae2ee2d7-a3e3-553b-9382-d96efbb553d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a16098d0-0dad-5305-9e69-e9a8a62050cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507025} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c16975e-f595-5cfb-a4f0-907d2ff52035" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1dd45167-c0b0-591e-9774-9fc65bcaba83" :question/math-academy-id "q-159681" :question/problem "![](images/bd/bdf61d89183886da1f519bad64221e7fdafd16a9ae5eb2c588728398f10477b5.png)\n\nWhat are the equations of the horizontal asymptotes of $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?" :question/worked-solution "- On the right of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets larger and larger, the function $f(x)$ becomes larger and larger without bound. We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty \n$$\n\nThis does not give us a horizontal asymptote.\n\n- On the left of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets more and more negative, the function $f(x)$ gets more and more negative without bound. We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to -\\infty \n$$\n\nThis does not give us a horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507492 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129243 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c271d00-a2fd-5665-bed0-f1f298296bd0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Missing Values in End Behavior Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50229f8f-47bc-5354-bb33-d1794e650241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-1,\\,b=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506860} {:answer/id #uuid "5b22833b-3851-5b09-8895-c0604eebe4e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-2,\\,b=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506893} {:answer/id #uuid "995515d4-16b8-5eaa-9cc4-aa9c72988636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-2,\\,b=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507011} {:answer/id #uuid "de7b0744-7060-50eb-85ac-16c37a35cbcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-\\infty ,\\,b=\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507160} {:answer/id #uuid "f3382fce-4cb4-531f-ac17-9bce1b6fd2d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=2,\\,b=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3382fce-4cb4-531f-ac17-9bce1b6fd2d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=2,\\,b=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507214} :answer-field/id #uuid "49dc3abe-2feb-54fb-b2bd-79cf0c5d99ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bfc547ca-ecd5-5977-a221-c751ecb9addf" :question/math-academy-id "q-51769" :question/problem "![](images/c0/c0da20fd092718bf477d9b00ee86f953c72c130e7da20ebbe9ae0fdb47705102.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find $a$ and $b$ such that $f(x)\\to a$ as $x\\to \\infty $ and $f(x)\\to b$ as $x\\to -\\infty .$" :question/worked-solution "- On the right of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets larger and larger, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $2.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 2\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2.$\n\n- On the left of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets more and more negative, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $-2.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to -2\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to -\\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore, $b=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507474 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129243 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c271d00-a2fd-5665-bed0-f1f298296bd0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Missing Values in End Behavior Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "085502e3-0844-581a-81a7-1029cd81abb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-\\infty ,\\,b=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506690} {:answer/id #uuid "7fa1da63-4f01-527e-8636-5d4e476ade73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=0,\\,b=-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506958} {:answer/id #uuid "87c5bf6d-f0a4-51d7-bab0-3adc093951ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-1,\\,b=\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506977} {:answer/id #uuid "a8990342-9d25-54f8-8c1b-ae3db05373e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-1,\\,b=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507046} {:answer/id #uuid "d4264719-7a66-559f-a145-ca916430398c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-\\infty ,\\,b=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fa1da63-4f01-527e-8636-5d4e476ade73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=0,\\,b=-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506958} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ba4e372-cbc2-50df-a9f6-159d0dbf1f66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6b3e916-f86a-5464-9d2a-5e30022c5b56" :question/math-academy-id "q-51770" :question/problem "![](images/1d/1d1712fb97a1a508a5603e055218a863eb314310a8af74cf6344a3cd7ab50731.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find $a$ and $b$ such that $f(x)\\to a$ as $x\\to \\infty $ and $f(x)\\to b$ as $x\\to -\\infty .$" :question/worked-solution "- On the right of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets larger and larger, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $0.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=0.$\n\n- On the left of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets more and more negative, the function $f(x)$ becomes more and more negative without bound. We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to -\\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore, $b=-\\infty .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507527 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129242 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "747f2317-3665-5857-9767-e602f711d6c6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Horizontal Asymptotes of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2198894d-6faa-527e-805a-e5aca91af8bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506744} {:answer/id #uuid "45ded6d1-96a4-52f4-a7c3-af64cb97ce2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506841} {:answer/id #uuid "7fdcf4a5-3618-5575-8eb1-65f8369c925c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506959} {:answer/id #uuid "b3294724-bbc4-5b22-a385-12259343cfb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507073} {:answer/id #uuid "b3979756-0c9a-57f0-bb7c-e44298a2202f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fdcf4a5-3618-5575-8eb1-65f8369c925c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506959} :answer-field/id #uuid "5da59dae-2cb3-527a-949a-c5154172b8de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f8c70775-4967-58d2-b21e-4833444c9363" :question/math-academy-id "q-77732" :question/problem "![](images/f2/f22c95e94846c80b0e1f6c0dc340da701f601a50a9a8908cde78d57825f38c89.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?" :question/worked-solution "On the left of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets more and more negative, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $-1.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to -1\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to -\\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a horizontal asymptote at $y=-1.$"}]}
